        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "After a small research I've found that:\n\n## Basedpyright - willRenameFiles\n\n- `willRenameFiles` -> fail -> `willRenameFiles` doesn't work to revert the text edits from the first `willRenameFiles`.\n- after the first `willRenameFiles`, even when the rename succeeded, the language server seems to be left in a broken state (it seems the modified import statement as an error). ~~This doesn't happen if the renamed file was previously opened in Neovim (my guess is that Basedpyright behaves like this because the [LSP spec](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.17/specification/#textDocument_didRename) mentions that renaming a file is signaled to a language server by a document close notification (with the old file name) followed by a document open notification (with the new file name).) This seems to be a bug on the Basedpyright side, but I think that the LSP spec may not be specific enough on how to handle renaming files.~~ Making this work seems to be more complicated that what I originally thought, now I can't reliably reproduce the state where the language server is not left in a broken state.\n\n### Filter\n\n```lua\n{ {\n  pattern = {\n    glob = \"**/*\",\n  },\n} }\n```\n## lua_ls - didRenameFiles\n\n- `didRenameFiles` -> fail -> `didRenameFiles` works to revert the text edits from the first `didRenameFiles`.\n- the language server behaves correctly no matter if the file being renamed was opened or not previously in a buffer.\n\n### Filter\n\n```lua\n{ {\n  pattern = {\n    glob = \"<root_dir>/**\",\n  },\n  ignoreCase = true\n} }\n```\n## typescript-tools - willRenameFiles\n\n- `willRenameFiles` -> fail -> `willRenameFiles` works to revert the text edits from the first `willRenameFiles`.\n- the language server behaves correctly no matter if the file being renamed was opened or not previously in a buffer. \n\n### Filter\n\n```lua\n{ {\n  pattern = {\n    glob = \"**/*.{ts,js,jsx,tsx,mjs,mts,cjs,cts}\",\n    matches = \"file\"\n  },\n  scheme = \"file\"\n}, {\n  pattern = {\n    glob = \"**/*\",\n    matches = \"folder\"\n  },\n  scheme = \"file\"\n} }\n```\n\nSo, for 2/3 language servers the \"ask to perform the opposite operation\" works. Also, it seems like `filter` is not really useful right now (?, but we should probably still use it.\n\n---\n\nBonus:\n \n## gopls - didCreateFiles\n\nIt uses the `didCreateFiles` notification to prepopulate a new file with `package package_name` on its first line. Since it doesn't support the `didDeleteFiles` method, trying to use it as the opposite operation of `didCreateFiles` won't work.",
